INT. OBSERVATORY - DOME ROOM - NIGHT

A round chamber of brass and iron. A huge telescope tilts toward a slit in the dome. Charts pinned to the walls. A cold teapot on a drafting table.

PROFESSOR ELLWOOD (60s, cardigan, ink-stained fingers) bends over a ledger, writing in small, careful figures. He hums.

The floor creaks on the stair. He doesn't look up.

ELLWOOD
Third night running.

Nobody answers. He turns a page.

ELLWOOD (CONT'D)
You needn't hover. If you've come about the missing plates, they're in the cabinet.

A hand appears on the railing. VELMA (30s, long coat, a thin leather satchel, hair damp from the rain) steps into the lamplight.

VELMA
Professor Ellwood?

ELLWOOD
That depends on who's asking.

VELMA
Velma Tran. From the Royal Society.

ELLWOOD
Ah.

He caps his pen. Rises. Smooths his cardigan.

ELLWOOD (CONT'D)
You're early.

VELMA
You were expecting me?

ELLWOOD
I was expecting someone. They always send a young person to deliver bad news.

VELMA
I'm not here to deliver bad news.

ELLWOOD
No?

VELMA
I'm here about the comet.

Ellwood pauses. He crosses to the telescope and runs a hand along its brass flank.

ELLWOOD
Which comet?

VELMA
The one you didn't report.

ELLWOOD
I report everything.

VELMA
Not this one.

She opens the satchel and lays three photographic plates on the drafting table beside the teapot. Ellwood doesn't look at them.

ELLWOOD
Those plates were stolen.

VELMA
They were mailed to us anonymously.

ELLWOOD
By whom?

VELMA
We were hoping you could tell us.

Ellwood chuckles softly and sits on the edge of the table.

ELLWOOD
Miss Tran, I have been observing this sky for forty years. I have discovered two comets, one nova, and a rather embarrassing asteroid that turned out to be a pigeon.

VELMA
That's a long career.

ELLWOOD
It has been a quiet one.

VELMA
Until August.

ELLWOOD
Until August.

He picks up the nearest plate and holds it to the lamp. A tiny streak crosses the black field.

ELLWOOD (CONT'D)
It was moving the wrong way.

VELMA
Yes.

ELLWOOD
Against everything we know about orbits.

VELMA
Yes.

ELLWOOD
So I assumed my instruments were broken.

VELMA
Were they?

ELLWOOD
No.

He places the plate down. Looks at her for a long time.

ELLWOOD (CONT'D)
Would you like tea?

VELMA
Is it hot?

ELLWOOD
It is not. But it is traditional.

Velma laughs, a short surprised sound. She takes off her damp coat and sets it on the chair.

VELMA
Yes, Professor. I would.
